/* Interactions shelf: original showpiece components. Each demo is one self-contained HTML file.
   uses: the Landing effects techniques (atlas.html#effects, effects-data.js) each piece is built from. */
window.INTERACTIONS=[
{id:'fan-deck',uses:['L49','L12'],t:'Fanned card deck',cat:'Cards',tone:'dark',d:'A stack of cards that fans into an arc when you approach, and brings the chosen card forward.',how:'Each card is absolutely positioned and rotated around a pivot below its base. Hovering or focusing the deck widens the spread; arrow keys move the active card.',use:'Portfolio covers, product variants, a team grid with personality.',p:'Build a fanned card deck in one HTML file with no libraries. Seven 4:5 image cards sit stacked with a slight offset. When the pointer enters or keyboard focus lands in the deck, the cards fan into an arc: each rotates around a pivot 20% below its bottom edge, spread evenly, with the active card lifted 34px. Clicking a card makes it active; Left/Right arrow keys change the active card. Use a 550ms cubic-bezier(.2,.8,.2,1) transition, dim inactive cards slightly, label each card for screen readers, and turn transitions off under prefers-reduced-motion.'},
{id:'arc-carousel',uses:['L30','L49'],t:'Arc carousel',cat:'Carousels',tone:'light',d:'Images ride a wide arc below the headline. Drag, scroll or use the arrows to turn it.',how:'Each image sits on a circle whose centre is below the screen. A single rotation value is driven by drag velocity and wheel input, with friction.',use:'Season lookbooks, collections, a landing page that feels hand-turned.',p:'Build an arc carousel in one HTML file, no libraries. Eighteen 4:5 images are placed on a circle whose centre is below the viewport (radius about 62% of the viewport width), so only the top of the arc is visible under a centred serif headline. A single rotation angle positions every image (x = sin(a)·R, y = −cos(a)·R) and each image is rotated to follow the curve, fading out past ±80°. Drag rotates with momentum and 0.94 friction; the mouse wheel and arrow keys add velocity; it drifts slowly when idle, except under prefers-reduced-motion.'},
{id:'ring-carousel',uses:['L08','L30'],t:'3D ring carousel',cat:'Carousels',tone:'dark',d:'Ten frames on a rotating cylinder, with drag momentum and step buttons.',how:'CSS 3D: each figure is rotated around the Y axis and pushed out by the ring radius. JavaScript only updates one angle.',use:'Galleries, product colourways, award shelves.',p:'Build a 3D ring carousel in one HTML file with CSS 3D transforms and vanilla JS. Ten 4:5 images sit on a cylinder: figure i gets rotateY(i·36°) translateZ(r), where r = width/2 / tan(π/10) + 24px. The ring has perspective 1100px and rotates with pointer drag, keeps momentum with 0.95 friction, and auto-rotates slowly when idle (not under prefers-reduced-motion). Add previous/next buttons that snap to the nearest frame, and arrow-key support on the focusable stage.'},
{id:'image-tunnel',uses:['L01','L08'],t:'Image tunnel',cat:'Scroll',tone:'dark',d:'Fly forward through a spiral of images. Scrolling speeds it up.',how:'Forty images are spread on a golden-angle spiral in 3D space and moved toward the camera every frame, wrapping when they pass it.',use:'Archive intros, festival line-ups, a dramatic “enter the site” moment.',p:'Build an image tunnel in one HTML file, no libraries. Forty images are positioned in 3D (perspective 600px) on a golden-angle spiral (angle = i·2.39996, radius 260–440px) at increasing depth. Every frame, each image moves toward the camera at a base speed plus a velocity added by wheel, touch drag and arrow keys (friction 0.92). Images wrap back to the far end when they pass the camera, fading in at the far end and out near the camera. Overlay a centred serif headline with mix-blend-mode: difference. Base speed is zero under prefers-reduced-motion.'},
{id:'particle-globe',uses:['L16'],t:'Particle globe',cat:'Canvas',tone:'dark',d:'1,400 points on a sphere. Drag to turn it; the pointer scatters nearby points.',how:'A Fibonacci sphere is rotated and projected onto a canvas each frame. Points near the pointer are pushed away and spring back.',use:'Global reach, network or community sections, a tech product hero.',p:'Build a particle globe on a full-screen canvas in one HTML file, no libraries. Place 1,400 points evenly on a unit sphere using the Fibonacci method. Each frame, rotate them by X and Y angles, project with simple perspective, and draw front-facing points brighter (cyan #6FD3FF) and back-facing points dim. Drag rotates the globe with inertia; idle rotation resumes gently. Front points within 120px of the pointer are pushed outward and ease back. Respect devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and stop idle rotation under prefers-reduced-motion. Add a short caption in the lower left.'},
{id:'clothesline',uses:['L03','L49'],t:'Clothesline gallery',cat:'Galleries',tone:'light',d:'Prints pegged to a sagging line. They swing as you scroll past.',how:'A horizontal scroller with an SVG cord. Each print has a damped spring angle; scroll speed and hover add impulses.',use:'Photography, illustration, studio notes, craft and maker brands.',p:'Build a clothesline gallery in one HTML file, no libraries. A horizontally scrolling row of white-bordered prints (polaroid style, 4:5 images with an italic caption) hangs from a gently sagging SVG cord drawn with quadratic curves; each print has a wooden peg. Each print rotates around its top centre with a damped spring (stiffness 0.04, damping 0.93, clamped to ±14°). Horizontal scroll speed and pointer hover push the springs so prints swing. Support mouse drag to scroll, keyboard scrolling on the focusable row, and no swinging under prefers-reduced-motion. Warm paper background.'},
{id:'folder-tabs',uses:['L49','L39'],t:'Folder tabs',cat:'Navigation',tone:'light',d:'Case-study chapters as manila folders. Tabs rise to meet you.',how:'A proper ARIA tab list. Panels are stacked; the selected one slides up and fades in while the others stay behind.',use:'Case studies, process pages, pricing tiers with a story.',p:'Build folder-style tabs in one HTML file, no libraries. Four tabs (Brief, Research, Direction, Delivery) look like manila folder tabs in four slightly different tan shades, sitting 6px low and rising on hover or selection. Their panels share the tab colour, have a two-column layout (serif heading and paragraph, plus a tilted photo) and stack on top of each other; the selected one slides up 24px and fades in. Use role=tablist/tab/tabpanel with roving tabindex, Left/Right/Home/End keys, and a single-column layout on phones. Turn off transitions under prefers-reduced-motion.'},
{id:'spotlight-list',uses:['L10','L29'],t:'Hover list with cursor image',cat:'Lists',tone:'light',d:'A big serif project list. Hovering a row floats its image under your pointer.',how:'One fixed preview follows the pointer with easing; rows switch which image is visible. The whole list dims except the hovered row.',use:'Agency work indexes, menus, archives, case-study lists.',p:'Build a project index with a cursor-follow image in one HTML file, no libraries. Show six rows, each with a number, a large serif title (clamp 26–58px) and a small category, separated by hairlines. Hovering the list dims all rows except the hovered one, which shifts right 12px. A fixed 4:5 image preview follows the pointer with 0.14 easing and a slight tilt based on velocity, cross-fading to the hovered row’s image. Hide the preview on touch devices (hover: none), keep keyboard focus styles, and remove easing under prefers-reduced-motion.'},
{id:'expanding-panels',uses:['L49','L13'],t:'Expanding panels',cat:'Galleries',tone:'dark',d:'Five tall panels; the one you choose grows to fill the row and shows its caption.',how:'A flex row where the open panel gets flex: 5. Closed panels show a vertical label; on phones the row becomes a stacked accordion.',use:'Services, featured work, chapter selectors.',p:'Build expanding image panels in one HTML file, no libraries. Five buttons sit in a flex row (height about 560px), each a full-bleed image with a dark bottom gradient. The open panel has flex: 5 and full colour; the others are desaturated and show their title rotated vertically. Open on hover (on hover-capable devices), focus and click, with aria-expanded. Animate flex over 600ms with cubic-bezier(.2,.8,.2,1) and fade the caption in after 250ms. Below 640px, switch to a vertical stack where closed panels are 64px tall and the open one 360px. No transitions under prefers-reduced-motion.'},
{id:'dial-slider',uses:['L49'],t:'Dial slider',cat:'Controls',tone:'dark',d:'Turn a physical-feeling knob to step through the studies.',how:'The pointer angle around the dial’s centre maps to a 270° sweep split into steps. It is an ARIA slider, so arrow keys and the wheel work too.',use:'Product configurators, time periods, mood or intensity pickers.',p:'Build a rotary dial slider in one HTML file, no libraries. A round dark knob with an orange indicator sits next to a 4:5 image viewer. Dragging around the dial maps the pointer angle (atan2 from the centre) onto a 270° sweep from −135°, snapping to eight steps marked with ticks around the edge (the active tick is longer and orange). Each step cross-fades the image and updates a title and subtitle in an aria-live region. Make the dial role=slider with aria-valuenow/valuetext and support arrow keys, Home/End and the mouse wheel. Stack the layout on phones.'},
{id:'quote-deck',uses:['L30','L49'],t:'Swipe quote deck',cat:'Cards',tone:'light',d:'Testimonials as a stack of cards. Throw the top one away to read the next.',how:'Cards are stacked with offset and scale. Dragging past 90px flings the top card and moves it to the back of the stack.',use:'Testimonials, tips, onboarding steps, FAQs.',p:'Build a swipeable testimonial deck in one HTML file, no libraries. Four cards (serif quote, avatar, name and role) are stacked: each card behind is 14px lower, 5% smaller and a slightly darker paper colour. Drag the top card sideways; it follows the pointer and rotates slightly. Release past 90px to fling it off and move it to the back of the stack, otherwise it springs back. Add Previous and Next buttons, mark hidden cards aria-hidden, and reduce motion to fades under prefers-reduced-motion.'},
{id:'halo-button',uses:['L09','L10'],t:'Halo magnetic button',cat:'Buttons',tone:'dark',d:'A pill button with a rotating gradient rim. It leans toward you, and its glow follows the pointer.',how:'A conic-gradient border made with a mask, a radial glow positioned by CSS variables, and a small magnetic translate.',use:'Primary calls to action on dark landing pages.',p:'Build a halo magnetic button in one HTML file, no libraries. A dark pill button has a 2px border made from a rotating conic gradient (violet #B48CFF to mint #5FE3C0) using the mask-composite technique, and a blurred radial glow behind it positioned at the pointer through CSS variables. Inside a larger hover zone, the button translates toward the pointer (25–30% of the offset) and its label moves a little more for depth; both spring back on leave. Stop the rotation and the movement under prefers-reduced-motion, and keep a visible focus outline.'},
{id:'watching-eyes',uses:['L09'],t:'Watching eyes button',cat:'Buttons',tone:'light',d:'A friendly button whose two eyes follow your pointer and blink now and then.',how:'Each pupil moves along the angle to the pointer, capped at 7px. A timer adds a short blink class at random intervals.',use:'Contact buttons, playful brands, 404 pages, kids’ products.',p:'Build a “watching eyes” button in one HTML file, no libraries. A dark pill button reads “Say hello” and starts with two white circular eyes. Each pupil moves toward the pointer anywhere on the page, along the angle from the eye centre, up to 7px. Every 2.2–5.2 seconds the eyes blink by squashing to 3px tall for 140ms (no blinking under prefers-reduced-motion). Add a gentle press scale, a visible focus outline, and mark the eyes aria-hidden.'},
{id:'tilt-row',uses:['L08','L10'],t:'Parallax tilt row',cat:'Cards',tone:'dark',d:'Cards that tilt toward the pointer while their image shifts the other way, with a soft glare.',how:'Pointer position inside each card sets rotateX/rotateY; the oversized image counter-moves; an overlay radial gradient tracks the pointer.',use:'Featured projects, product tiles, team members.',p:'Build a row of parallax tilt cards in one HTML file, no libraries. Four 4:5 image cards sit in a responsive grid with perspective 900px. On pointer move, each card rotates up to ±7° toward the pointer, its oversized image (116%) moves the opposite way by up to 18px, and an overlay-blended radial glare follows the pointer. The title sits forward with translateZ(40px). Ease back on leave. Under prefers-reduced-motion, keep only the glare.'},
{id:'zoom-reveal',uses:['L04','L19'],t:'Zoom-through reveal',cat:'Scroll',tone:'light',d:'One framed image grows to fill the screen as you scroll, while the words beside it step aside.',how:'A tall section with a sticky stage. Scroll progress, eased, sets the frame’s width, height and corner radius, and pushes the two words outward.',use:'Hero-to-story transitions, product reveals, film launches.',p:'Build a scroll-driven zoom reveal in one HTML file, no libraries. A 320vh section holds a sticky full-screen stage. At the start a 4:5 framed image (30% of the width, 14px radius) sits between two large serif words. As the visitor scrolls through the section, eased progress grows the frame to the full viewport, removes the radius, settles the image scale from 1.2 to 1, slides the words off to either side and fades in a caption at the end. Hide the words below 600px. Under prefers-reduced-motion, jump between the start and end states halfway.'},
{id:'scroll-index',uses:['L07'],t:'Scroll index',cat:'Navigation',tone:'light',d:'A sticky chapter index that highlights where you are, with a progress bar.',how:'An IntersectionObserver with a thin centre band marks the current chapter; the bar scales with page progress.',use:'Long reads, case studies, documentation, annual reports.',p:'Build a sticky scroll index in one HTML file, no libraries. A left column holds a numbered list of five chapter links, vertically centred and sticky; the main column has one tall section per chapter (serif heading, paragraph, image). An IntersectionObserver with rootMargin −45% top and bottom marks the current chapter: its link darkens, its title shifts 8px right and it gets aria-current. A thin bar under the list fills with overall scroll progress. Links scroll smoothly (instantly under prefers-reduced-motion). On phones, the index becomes a horizontal sticky strip at the top.'},
{id:'mesh-lines',uses:['L16'],t:'Flowing line mesh',cat:'Canvas',tone:'dark',d:'Sixty lines drift through a noise field behind the headline. The pointer bends them.',how:'Each line is sampled at 80 points and offset by layered sine noise over time. Points near the eased pointer are pushed away radially.',use:'Hero backgrounds for audio, fintech, research or AI products.',p:'Build a flowing line-mesh background on a full-screen canvas in one HTML file, no libraries. Draw 60 horizontal lines from 12% to 88% of the height, each sampled at 80 points and displaced by layered sine “noise” that moves slowly over time. An eased pointer position pushes nearby points away within a 180px radius. Colour the lines in blue-to-violet hues with alpha peaking in the middle lines, and leave a faint trail by painting a translucent background each frame. Put a centred serif headline and a short line of copy on top. Freeze time under prefers-reduced-motion.'},
{id:'story-tidewater',uses:['L02','L03','L01'],t:'Tidewater: a coastal hotel',cat:'Scroll stories',tone:'dark',d:'A full one-page site built around the bioluminescent film: hero zoom, words that light up as you read, a corridor of rooms scrolled sideways, and a night itinerary beside a pinned film.',how:'Scroll progress drives the hero scale, the word highlighting and the sideways room track inside a sticky section. IntersectionObservers light the itinerary steps and play films only while they are on screen.',use:'Hotels, retreats, restaurants, any place people visit.',p:'Build a one-page cinematic hotel site in one HTML file with no libraries (Google Fonts allowed: Fraunces and Inter). Palette: deep sea #06141A, glow teal #5FE0D0, warm sand #EFE6D6. Sections: (1) a fixed nav that turns translucent after the hero; (2) a full-screen looping background video hero with a large serif headline that fades and rises as you scroll while the video scales up 15%; (3) a manifesto paragraph split into words that light up from 16% to 100% opacity as scroll progress passes through it; (4) a 340vh “Rooms” section with a sticky stage where a row of five room cards (image, name, price) moves sideways with vertical scroll; (5) a two-column “night” section with a sticky looping video on one side and three timed steps that brighten as each crosses the middle of the screen; (6) a booking form (date, nights, guests) that shows a confirmation message without sending anything. Lazy-load and pause off-screen videos, stack to one column on phones, and under prefers-reduced-motion pause the videos and show every state without animation.'},
{id:'story-gilt',uses:['L33','L02'],t:'Gilt: a gold-leaf atelier',cat:'Scroll stories',tone:'dark',d:'A luxury brand page where the name is a window onto the film, then three chapters swap between the golden dove, the gold figure and the lion.',how:'A black layer with white type uses mix-blend-mode: multiply, so the film only shows through the letters. It scales and fades with scroll. Chapters cross-fade a fixed stack of films.',use:'Jewellery, fashion, perfume, craft studios, premium launches.',p:'Build a one-page luxury atelier site in one HTML file with no libraries (Google Fonts: Bodoni Moda and Manrope). Three looping gold films sit stacked in a fixed full-screen layer. The opening is a 200vh section with a sticky black panel showing the brand name in huge white serif type, with mix-blend-mode: multiply so the film shows only through the letters; as you scroll, the word scales up 2.4× and the panel fades to reveal the full film. Then three tall chapter sections (small gold numeral label, italic serif heading, paragraph) each fade in and cross-fade the background to their own film over 1.2s, with a dark gradient for legibility. Then a four-item product grid with slow image zoom on hover, a cream “letter” section with an outlined button, and a footer. Only the active film plays and others load lazily. Under prefers-reduced-motion, films stay paused on their posters.'},
{id:'story-fieldnotes',uses:['L04','L27','L02'],t:'Field Notes: a research studio',cat:'Scroll stories',tone:'light',d:'An editorial, paper-toned page. The marbling film opens from a small circle to the full screen, numbers count up, and a pinned plate changes with each note.',how:'Scroll progress eases a clip-path circle from 14% to 76%. Counters run once when seen. An observer on each note swaps the image on the sticky plate.',use:'Studios, research groups, publications, portfolios with a method.',p:'Build an editorial one-page studio site in one HTML file with no libraries (Google Fonts: Instrument Serif, IBM Plex Sans, IBM Plex Mono). Paper #F2EEE6 with ink #1D1A16 and hairline rules. Open with a 260vh section whose sticky stage shows a looping video clipped to a circle (clip-path: circle) that grows from 14% to 76% with smoothstep-eased scroll progress, while a large serif headline fades away and a caption fades in at the end. Then a three-column stats band with numbers that count up once when visible. Then a two-column “method” section: a sticky image plate on the left that cross-fades between four images as each of four note entries (mono label, serif heading, paragraph, material tags) crosses the middle of the viewport. End with an index list of further notes. Stack on phones and skip animation under prefers-reduced-motion.'},
{id:'story-signal',uses:['L12','L34','L14'],t:'Signal: a product launch',cat:'Scroll stories',tone:'dark',d:'A SaaS launch page with a film hero, a bento grid that assembles as you scroll, counting stats, pricing with a gradient-edged plan and a film close.',how:'Bento cells fade up in sequence with staggered delays. Videos inside cells load only when needed. The highlighted plan uses a padding-box and border-box gradient trick.',use:'Software launches, apps, AI tools, developer products.',p:'Build a dark SaaS launch page in one HTML file with no libraries (Google Fonts: Space Grotesk and Inter). Colours: near-black #05070B, panels #0C1119, accents cyan #7CF0FF and violet #A98BFF. Include: a floating pill nav with a solid “Start free” button; a full-screen hero with a looping background video under a radial vignette, a status pill, a large headline with a gradient-text phrase, and two buttons; a row of wordmark logos; a six-column bento grid (one large 4×2 video cell, smaller text cells, one with an animated bar meter, two with images) whose cells fade and rise in sequence when scrolled into view; a stats row whose numbers count up once; three pricing cards where the middle one has a gradient border made with padding-box/border-box backgrounds; and a closing call to action over a second video. Lazy-load and pause off-screen videos, use a single column below 820px, and turn off animation under prefers-reduced-motion.'},
{id:'sec-pricing',uses:['L27','L49'],t:'Pricing with billing toggle',cat:'Sections',tone:'light',hash:'paper',d:'Three plans with a monthly/yearly switch, a highlighted middle plan and tick lists.',how:'A segmented toggle swaps each price from data attributes. The featured plan is lifted, outlined in the accent colour and tagged. The file carries four styles (Kitchen noir, Kitchen paper, Studio, Signal) as CSS variables; switch with data-style on the html element, or delete the ones you do not need.',use:'SaaS, memberships, courses, studios with packages.',p:'Build a pricing section in one HTML file with no libraries. A centred kicker, serif heading and intro, then a pill-shaped Monthly/Yearly toggle (aria-pressed) that swaps the prices stored in data-m and data-y attributes and the “/ month, billed yearly” note. Three plan cards in an auto-fit grid: name, one-line description, large serif price, a tick list and a full-width button. The middle plan is lifted 8px, has a 2px accent border, a soft accent shadow and a “Most chosen” tag. Define four interchangeable styles with CSS custom properties on [data-style] (noir: charcoal #16120F, cream #F3EADB, saffron #E2A24A, Fraunces + DM Sans; paper: cream #F6F0E6, ink #231C15, caramel #9A5B12; studio: #F4F5F8, ink #1C1F2A, violet #5B2DFF, italic Cormorant Garamond + Inter; signal: #05070B, #EAF0F8, cyan #7CF0FF, Space Grotesk + Inter) and a small fixed style switcher. Respect prefers-reduced-motion and keep every control keyboard-accessible.'},
{id:'sec-bento',uses:['L12'],t:'Bento feature grid',cat:'Sections',tone:'dark',hash:'signal',d:'A six-column bento with one large image tile and five feature tiles that each show something: a stat, chips, a bar chart, a numeral, a tick.',how:'CSS grid with spans (4×2 for the lead tile, 2×1 for the rest), collapsing to two columns and then one. The file carries four styles (Kitchen noir, Kitchen paper, Studio, Signal) as CSS variables; switch with data-style on the html element, or delete the ones you do not need.',use:'Product features, service overviews, “why us” sections.',p:'Build a bento feature grid in one HTML file with no libraries. A heading row with a kicker, serif heading and a short paragraph aligned to the right. Below, a six-column grid with 190px minimum rows: one 4×2 tile with a full-bleed image fading into the surface colour at the bottom, plus five 2×1 tiles each led by a small visual (a big accent statistic, a row of tag chips, a six-bar chart, a large numeral, a tick) above a title and one line of copy. Tiles lift 3px and take an accent border on hover. Two columns under 820px, one under 520px. Define four interchangeable styles with CSS custom properties on [data-style] (noir: charcoal #16120F, cream #F3EADB, saffron #E2A24A, Fraunces + DM Sans; paper: cream #F6F0E6, ink #231C15, caramel #9A5B12; studio: #F4F5F8, ink #1C1F2A, violet #5B2DFF, italic Cormorant Garamond + Inter; signal: #05070B, #EAF0F8, cyan #7CF0FF, Space Grotesk + Inter) and a small fixed style switcher. Respect prefers-reduced-motion and keep every control keyboard-accessible.'},
{id:'sec-testimonials',uses:['L11'],t:'Scrolling testimonial wall',cat:'Sections',tone:'dark',hash:'noir',d:'Three columns of quote cards drifting at different speeds, with a fade at the top and bottom edges.',how:'Each column holds its cards twice and translates −50% in a linear loop, so the motion is seamless. Hover, focus or the button pauses it; the copies are hidden from screen readers. The file carries four styles (Kitchen noir, Kitchen paper, Studio, Signal) as CSS variables; switch with data-style on the html element, or delete the ones you do not need.',use:'Social proof, reviews, community quotes.',p:'Build a testimonial wall in one HTML file with no libraries. Under a centred heading, a 620px-tall area holds three columns of quote cards (stars, serif headline, quote, avatar, name and channel). Each column contains its cards twice and animates translateY(0 → −50%) in a linear infinite loop at 40s, 52s (reversed) and 46s. Mask the top and bottom edges with a linear-gradient mask. Pause on hover or focus, and add a Pause/Play button with aria-pressed. Mark the duplicated cards aria-hidden. Show two columns under 860px and one under 560px. Define four interchangeable styles with CSS custom properties on [data-style] (noir: charcoal #16120F, cream #F3EADB, saffron #E2A24A, Fraunces + DM Sans; paper: cream #F6F0E6, ink #231C15, caramel #9A5B12; studio: #F4F5F8, ink #1C1F2A, violet #5B2DFF, italic Cormorant Garamond + Inter; signal: #05070B, #EAF0F8, cyan #7CF0FF, Space Grotesk + Inter) and a small fixed style switcher. Respect prefers-reduced-motion and keep every control keyboard-accessible.'},
{id:'sec-faq',uses:['L49','L46'],t:'FAQ accordion',cat:'Sections',tone:'light',hash:'studio',d:'A two-column FAQ: a heading with a contact button on one side, and native details/summary questions with a smooth open on the other.',how:'Native details elements, so it works without JavaScript. The answer animates open with a grid-template-rows 0fr → 1fr transition, and the plus icon turns into a filled minus. The file carries four styles (Kitchen noir, Kitchen paper, Studio, Signal) as CSS variables; switch with data-style on the html element, or delete the ones you do not need.',use:'Help sections, pricing pages, onboarding.',p:'Build an FAQ section in one HTML file using native <details>/<summary> elements. Left column: kicker, serif heading, a line about response times and an “Ask us” button. Right column: five questions separated by hairlines. Hide the default marker; draw a round plus icon with pseudo-elements that turns into a filled accent minus when open. Wrap each answer in a grid whose grid-template-rows transitions from 0fr to 1fr so it slides open smoothly. One column under 760px. Define four interchangeable styles with CSS custom properties on [data-style] (noir: charcoal #16120F, cream #F3EADB, saffron #E2A24A, Fraunces + DM Sans; paper: cream #F6F0E6, ink #231C15, caramel #9A5B12; studio: #F4F5F8, ink #1C1F2A, violet #5B2DFF, italic Cormorant Garamond + Inter; signal: #05070B, #EAF0F8, cyan #7CF0FF, Space Grotesk + Inter) and a small fixed style switcher. Respect prefers-reduced-motion and keep every control keyboard-accessible.'},
{id:'sec-contact',uses:['L46'],t:'Contact form',cat:'Sections',tone:'light',hash:'paper',d:'A friendly contact section with direct details, toggle chips for the topic and inline validation messages.',how:'Validation runs on blur and on submit, with messages tied to each field through aria-describedby and aria-invalid. The chips are real checkboxes styled with :has(). The file carries four styles (Kitchen noir, Kitchen paper, Studio, Signal) as CSS variables; switch with data-style on the html element, or delete the ones you do not need.',use:'Studios, agencies, consultants, any “get in touch” page.',p:'Build a contact section in one HTML file with no libraries. Left: kicker, serif heading, a short reassuring line and two detail cards (email and studio address, each with an outline icon, the value selectable as text). Right: a card form with name and email side by side, a fieldset of topic chips (real checkboxes styled with :has(input:checked) to fill with the accent colour) and a message textarea. Validate on blur and on submit with specific messages under each field linked by aria-describedby and aria-invalid; focus the first invalid field. On success show a status message instead of sending anything. Define four interchangeable styles with CSS custom properties on [data-style] (noir: charcoal #16120F, cream #F3EADB, saffron #E2A24A, Fraunces + DM Sans; paper: cream #F6F0E6, ink #231C15, caramel #9A5B12; studio: #F4F5F8, ink #1C1F2A, violet #5B2DFF, italic Cormorant Garamond + Inter; signal: #05070B, #EAF0F8, cyan #7CF0FF, Space Grotesk + Inter) and a small fixed style switcher. Respect prefers-reduced-motion and keep every control keyboard-accessible.'},
{id:'sec-footer',uses:['L01','L19'],t:'Big wordmark footer',cat:'Sections',tone:'dark',hash:'noir',d:'A footer with a newsletter form, three link columns and a giant wordmark that rises into place as it scrolls into view.',how:'Scroll position inside the viewport sets a progress variable; the wordmark and its accent full stop translate up at slightly different rates. The file carries four styles (Kitchen noir, Kitchen paper, Studio, Signal) as CSS variables; switch with data-style on the html element, or delete the ones you do not need.',use:'Brand sites, studios, product marketing pages.',p:'Build a large footer in one HTML file with no libraries. A surface-coloured footer with a top hairline holds a four-column grid: a newsletter block (serif heading, email input and Join button, reassurance line, status on submit), then Product, Kitchen and Follow link lists with small uppercase headings. Underneath, the brand name as a huge serif wordmark (clamp 80–300px, line-height .78, tight tracking) with a full stop in the accent colour; as the footer scrolls into view, a 0–1 progress value moves the word up from 40% and the dot from 30%. Finish with a legal row. Two columns under 820px. Define four interchangeable styles with CSS custom properties on [data-style] (noir: charcoal #16120F, cream #F3EADB, saffron #E2A24A, Fraunces + DM Sans; paper: cream #F6F0E6, ink #231C15, caramel #9A5B12; studio: #F4F5F8, ink #1C1F2A, violet #5B2DFF, italic Cormorant Garamond + Inter; signal: #05070B, #EAF0F8, cyan #7CF0FF, Space Grotesk + Inter) and a small fixed style switcher. Respect prefers-reduced-motion and keep every control keyboard-accessible.'},
{id:'sec-logos',uses:['L11'],t:'Logo cloud marquee',cat:'Sections',tone:'light',hash:'studio',d:'Two rows of client marks sliding in opposite directions, with soft edges and a pause button.',how:'Each row duplicates its logos and slides −50% in a linear loop. The second copy is aria-hidden, and hovering a row pauses it. The file carries four styles (Kitchen noir, Kitchen paper, Studio, Signal) as CSS variables; switch with data-style on the html element, or delete the ones you do not need.',use:'Client lists, partners, press, integrations.',p:'Build a logo marquee section in one HTML file with no libraries. A heading row with a kicker, a short serif heading and a Pause button (aria-pressed). Below, two rows of pill-shaped logo tiles (simple outline icon plus wordmark in sans, serif or mono to vary the look). Each row contains its tiles twice and translates −50% in a linear loop (38s, and 46s reversed for the second row), with a horizontal gradient mask fading both edges. Pause a row on hover; the button pauses both. Mark the duplicate set aria-hidden. Define four interchangeable styles with CSS custom properties on [data-style] (noir: charcoal #16120F, cream #F3EADB, saffron #E2A24A, Fraunces + DM Sans; paper: cream #F6F0E6, ink #231C15, caramel #9A5B12; studio: #F4F5F8, ink #1C1F2A, violet #5B2DFF, italic Cormorant Garamond + Inter; signal: #05070B, #EAF0F8, cyan #7CF0FF, Space Grotesk + Inter) and a small fixed style switcher. Respect prefers-reduced-motion and keep every control keyboard-accessible.'},
{id:'sec-cta',uses:['L14','L26'],t:'Film call-to-action banner',cat:'Sections',tone:'dark',hash:'signal',d:'A rounded banner with a looping film behind a gradient, a big headline and two buttons, plus a pause control.',how:'The gradient is mixed from the page background with color-mix, so it adapts to every style. A real pause button controls the film. The file carries four styles (Kitchen noir, Kitchen paper, Studio, Signal) as CSS variables; switch with data-style on the html element, or delete the ones you do not need.',use:'End-of-page calls to action, launches, sign-ups.',p:'Build a call-to-action banner in one HTML file with no libraries. A large rounded container (min-height 440px) has a looping muted background video and a left-to-right gradient made with color-mix from the page background colour so the copy stays readable. On top: kicker, large serif headline, a line of copy and two buttons. Add a round pause/play button in the top right with an accurate aria-label. On phones, switch the gradient to bottom-to-top and increase the height. Under prefers-reduced-motion, start paused on the poster. Define four interchangeable styles with CSS custom properties on [data-style] (noir: charcoal #16120F, cream #F3EADB, saffron #E2A24A, Fraunces + DM Sans; paper: cream #F6F0E6, ink #231C15, caramel #9A5B12; studio: #F4F5F8, ink #1C1F2A, violet #5B2DFF, italic Cormorant Garamond + Inter; signal: #05070B, #EAF0F8, cyan #7CF0FF, Space Grotesk + Inter) and a small fixed style switcher. Respect prefers-reduced-motion and keep every control keyboard-accessible.'},
{id:"status-header",uses:["L18"],t:"Status header with discreet login",cat:"Signal & data",tone:"dark",d:"Logo top left, a live monospace status line top right, quiet nav, a sound toggle, a discreet login and a small Apply pill that follows you.",how:"A fixed header with pointer-events only on its children. Once the page scrolls, a graphite gradient fades in behind it. The status text and its spoken label come from CONFIG; the pulse dot stops under reduced motion.",use:"Showcase sites, AI and infrastructure products, any page where the headline should own the screen.",p:"Build a fixed site header in one HTML file. Left: a bold text wordmark with a small cyan square as the dot. Right, stacked: a monospace uppercase status line (e.g. \"SERVER v1.0 // NODE: WAR ROOM // UPTIME 99.999%\") with a pulsing green dot, then a nav of 12px monospace uppercase links, a sound toggle button (aria-pressed) and a muted \"Login\" link. When scrollY > 40, fade in a dark gradient backdrop behind the header. Add a fixed pill \"Apply\" link bottom right with a glowing cyan square. Graphite #07090C background, platinum #E7EAEE text, Geist and Geist Mono. On phones hide the status line, shrink the links and keep the sound label for screen readers only. Include a skip link and respect prefers-reduced-motion."},
{id:"circuit-city",uses:["L43","L16"],t:"Circuit city hero (3D)",cat:"Signal & data",tone:"dark",d:"A motherboard laid out like a city: chips are towers, traces are streets, light is traffic that never stops. Angled camera, pointer parallax, scroll moves you deeper in.",how:"three.js r128 from cdnjs. Towers are boxes with canvas-drawn facades (lit cells as windows, pins at the foot). Traces are Manhattan routes with 45° chamfers drawn as additive ribbons; 2,000 points of light ride them. Fog gives depth. The camera lerps between two angled positions as you scroll. Seed, counts, fog, camera and every colour are in CONFIG.",use:"The hero for an AI, data or infrastructure product; any brief that asks for scale, power and constant activity.",p:"Build a full-screen three.js (r128) hero of a circuit board seen as a city at night, in one HTML file. Generate a seeded city: up to 190 chips as box \"towers\" on a 4-unit grid, most low, a few tall near the centre, with canvas textures (dark facades with rows of lit cyan-white cells, a row of pins at the foot, a labelled chip top). Draw traces as Manhattan routes with 45-degree chamfers plus a few long bright trunk avenues, as flat additive ribbons in teal #2C8FA0 and cyan #7FE7F7. Animate 2,000 glowing points travelling along the routes (mostly white-cyan, rare green, purple and amber). Add blinking green LEDs on chip tops and FogExp2 in graphite #07090C. Keep the camera angled and off-centre, never overhead: lerp it from (34,15,40) looking at (-6,0,-14) to (16,7,6) looking at (-26,1,-46) as the page scrolls, with gentle pointer parallax. Pause when the tab is hidden, render one still frame under prefers-reduced-motion, and add a pause button. Overlay a big left-aligned headline with a scrim that darkens the left side."},
{id:"binary-helix",uses:["L16","L05"],t:"Binary helix",cat:"Signal & data",tone:"dark",d:"A faint, small double helix built from 0s and 1s. It unzips, each half grows a new partner, and one strand leaves as a new agent.",how:"A 2D canvas draws two strands of glyphs on sine paths, with depth from size and opacity. A ten-second cycle unzips from the top, decodes new partner glyphs in, slides the second helix away and fires onSpawn. It sits in a clipped box behind the copy, rotated -14°, at low opacity.",use:"An agents or \"how it learns\" page, an AI product backdrop. Keep it a whisper behind the copy, not the centrepiece.",p:"Build a subtle binary DNA helix on a canvas in one HTML file. Draw 30 base pairs as two strands of \"0\"/\"1\" glyphs in Geist Mono, positioned with x = centre ± amplitude·sin(phase + i·0.56), sized and faded by depth (cos). Join pairs with faint cyan rungs. Every 10 seconds: unzip from top to bottom (rungs fade, strands drift apart), grow a complementary partner beside each half (glyphs decode in from random bits), slide the right-hand helix away and fade it, then settle; flip a few bits for a mutation and call onSpawn(). Keep it small (220×560px), rotated about -14 degrees, at about 0.6 opacity, positioned to the right behind the headline, inside an overflow-hidden box. Draw a single still frame under prefers-reduced-motion. Show a status line that updates on each spawn."},
{id:"telemetry-feeds",uses:["L27","L16"],t:"Live telemetry feeds",cat:"Signal & data",tone:"dark",d:"A war-room strip: a sparkline, stats that tick, a scrolling event log and a live counter, in corner-tick frames rather than cards.",how:"Timers update a canvas sparkline (220ms), the log (900ms), the stats (1.2s) and the counter (2.6s). Bars move in steps(6). Events are [action, field, value()] triples in CONFIG. Everything stays static under reduced motion.",use:"Under a hero, on dashboards and status pages, anywhere the product should feel alive.",p:"Build a row of three live telemetry panels in one HTML file, framed only by two corner ticks each (top-left and bottom-right), never full boxes. Panel 1: a cyan sparkline on canvas that shifts left every 220ms with a green head, plus CPU and memory percentages with thin green bars that move in steps(6) and a node count that drifts. Panel 2: an event log in Geist Mono, newest at the bottom, max 7 lines, each \"[hh:mm:ss] AGENT_1234 >> ACTION [ FIELD value ]\" with the action in cyan and the value in green, inside a role=\"log\" wrapper. Panel 3: a big monospace counter that occasionally increments. Graphite background, platinum text. Stack to two columns then one on small screens, and keep everything still under prefers-reduced-motion."},
{id:"routed-motion",uses:["L05","L27"],t:"Routed motion kit",cat:"Signal & data",tone:"dark",d:"Four motion primitives with their code: Route (arrive along a trace), Lock (a hard-settling ease), Decode (text resolves from 0s and 1s) and Tick (numbers count in steps).",how:"Small functions you can lift: decode(el) scrambles from a noise alphabet left to right and gives screen readers the final text; tick(el, n) counts in six jumps; route(line, el, distance) draws a line then moves the element along it; the lock ease is cubic-bezier(.2,.9,.1,1). Timing lives in CONFIG.",use:"A motion language for a technical brand: headlines, counters, panels arriving, reveals on scroll.",p:"Build a motion-primitives page in one HTML file with four demos, each with a Replay button and an IntersectionObserver that plays it once on view. Route: a 1px trace draws across (320ms, cubic-bezier(.6,0,.2,1)), then a chip slides along it and locks (320ms, cubic-bezier(.2,.9,.1,1)). Lock: an SVG of that ease curve with a dot riding it. Decode: a headline that resolves left to right, 14ms per character, with the next 8 characters as random 0/1 noise, setting aria-label to the final text while it runs. Tick: a number that counts to 1,042 in six visible steps. Export the functions decode(), tick() and route() cleanly with a CONFIG object for the timings, and make every effect instant under prefers-reduced-motion."},
{id:"machine-sounds",uses:["L46"],t:"Machine sound kit",cat:"Signal & data",tone:"dark",d:"Six tiny interface sounds (tick, click, chirp, confirm, buzz, deny) synthesised in the browser. No audio files; off until the visitor turns them on.",how:"Web Audio oscillators and a filtered noise burst with short attack/decay envelopes. Sound.set(true) is only called from a click, and the choice is saved in localStorage. Each sound is one line you can retune.",use:"Products that should feel like instruments: hover ticks, a chirp when something new arrives, a buzz when something is refused.",p:"Build a synthesised UI sound kit in one HTML file with the Web Audio API, no audio files. Sounds: tick (12ms white noise through a 5.2kHz high-pass), click (30ms 2.4kHz sine), chirp (60ms sine sweep 3.2 to 6.4kHz), confirm (two short sines at 1.6 then 2.4kHz), buzz (90ms 118Hz sawtooth through a 900Hz band-pass), deny (80ms square 220 to 180Hz). Use short linear attack and exponential decay envelopes at low gain. Sound starts off; a toggle (aria-pressed) turns it on from a user gesture and saves the choice in localStorage. List each sound with a play button, a one-line use and its recipe, and add three buttons that tick on hover, confirm on press and buzz on press."},
{id:"integration-wall",uses:["L13"],t:"Integration logo wall",cat:"Signal & data",tone:"dark",d:"76 real integration marks on one hairline grid, with category filters, search, a tick-up count and live dots that move around.",how:"Marks are Simple Icons SVG paths (CC0) stored as {slug, title, category, path}. The grid is drawn from that list, so filtering and search just re-render it with a short stagger. A few cells get a green live dot that reshuffles every 2.4 seconds.",use:"An integrations page, a \"works with\" section, a partner wall.",p:"Build an integrations wall in one HTML file. Store marks as an array of {s, t, c, p} (slug, title, category, SVG path from Simple Icons, CC0). Show a huge tick-up count (\"70+\"), a headline, category filter buttons with counts (aria-pressed) and a search field. Render a grid of square cells with shared 1px hairlines (no cards): each cell has the mark at 34px in platinum and the name in small monospace caps. On hover the mark turns cyan-white, lifts 2px and a cyan line draws across the top. Mark about 14 random cells with a glowing green \"live\" dot that reshuffles every few seconds, and explain it with a legend. Animate re-renders with a 14ms stagger, announce the result count politely, and credit Simple Icons and the trademark owners."},
{id:"follow-the-sun",uses:["L27"],t:"Follow-the-sun board",cat:"Signal & data",tone:"dark",d:"Your teams' working hours on a 24-hour board with a live \"now\" line. Lanes light green while a team is working, and the board reports the coverage.",how:"Each team is [name, place, UTC offset, start, end]. Local hours become UTC bars, wrapping past midnight. Every 30 seconds the now line moves, working lanes turn green and the coverage is recounted.",use:"An origin or about page (\"100+ people on 4 continents\"), support coverage, a distributed team.",p:"Build a follow-the-sun board in one HTML file. From a CONFIG list of teams (name, city, UTC offset, local start and end hour) draw one lane each on a shared 24-hour UTC axis with labels at 00, 06, 12, 18 and 24. Convert local hours to UTC bars, splitting any that wrap past midnight. Draw a thin vertical \"NOW hh:mm UTC\" line at the current time; bars for teams working right now glow green, the others are soft cyan. Under the board, report \"Coverage N / 24 hours · X of Y working right now\". Give each lane an accessible label. Update every 30 seconds and on resize, and stack the labels above the lanes on phones."},
{id:"hairline-lists",uses:["L24","L19"],t:"Hairline claims and roster",cat:"Signal & data",tone:"dark",d:"Big numbered claims on hairlines with a few words in the signal colour, and a roster with spec columns and a trace that draws under the hovered row. No cards anywhere.",how:"Both lists are drawn from arrays in the file. Wrap words in *stars* to colour them. Rows fade up and lock into place as they enter view; on hover or focus a cyan line runs along the bottom edge.",use:"\"Why us\" claims, product tiers, a team or agent roster, specs: anywhere you would reach for a grid of cards.",p:"Build two hairline lists in one HTML file, with no cards or boxes. First, a claims list: numbered rows (01–06) with a huge statement (30–68px, tight tracking), certain words coloured cyan, and a small monospace caption right-aligned, separated by 1px hairlines. Second, a roster: rows with a code (AG-01), a large name, a one-line description and a right-aligned monospace spec block (labels muted, values green). On hover or keyboard focus a cyan line draws along the bottom of the row in 320ms. Build both from arrays, reveal rows with a short fade-up on scroll, and collapse to fewer columns on small screens."},
{id:"staged-application",uses:["L13","L39"],t:"Long staged application",cat:"Signal & data",tone:"dark",d:"A long, demanding six-stage application with a progress rail, minimum lengths with live counters, autosave on the device, a review step and a send that falls back to copy or download.",how:"Stages and fields are data ({k, label, type, req, min, opts}). Each stage validates before you can move on and focuses the first problem. Answers save to localStorage as you type. On send it POSTs {answers, sections} to CONFIG.endpoint; with no endpoint, or on failure, the applicant can copy the text or download it. The atlas repo has api/apply.js, which emails it through Resend.",use:"Applications, waitlists that should feel exclusive, onboarding questionnaires, grant forms.",p:"Build a multi-stage application form in one HTML file, defined as data: stages, each with fields {k, label, type: text|email|textarea|select|date|checks|oath, req, min, opts, hint}. Show a sticky vertical progress rail of numbered stages on the left (completed stages green, the current one cyan, later ones disabled) and one stage at a time on the right with Back and Continue. Textareas show live \"n / min minimum\" counters that turn green when met. Continue validates the stage, writes errors under each field with aria-invalid, focuses the first problem and blocks moving on. Autosave everything to localStorage and restore it on load. The final stage is a full review; Send POSTs JSON {answers, sections} to a configurable endpoint. If the send fails or no endpoint is set, offer Copy and Download as .txt. Include a hidden honeypot field. Underline-only inputs, graphite background, platinum type."},
{id:"agent-console",uses:["L05","L27","L16"],t:"Agent console",cat:"Signal & data",tone:"dark",d:"An agentic chat interface: models light up along a route, a swarm of agents spawns, tool calls stream in with their icons, then the answer streams word by word. Scripted demo runs.",how:"Three panes: sessions, the conversation and a live route panel (models with share bars, a 96-cell swarm grid, counters and a clock). Each run is data {q, agents, route, tools, body}, played in four phases with short waits. Free text picks the nearest run or a generic one, and the screen says the responses are scripted.",use:"Showing off an AI product before the real backend exists, pitch demos, a product tour.",p:"Build an agent chat console in one HTML file with three columns: a sessions sidebar (new session button, grouped history, agents online), the conversation (a top bar labelled \"Demo · scripted responses\", suggested prompts as hairline rows, a composer with an auto-growing textarea where Enter sends) and a live route panel (models with progress bars, a 16×6 swarm grid, and counters for models, agents, tools and elapsed time). Playing a run: 1) the route renders as a chain of model chips joined by lines that light cyan one by one; 2) swarm cells light cyan as agents spawn; 3) tool calls appear as monospace lines with the tool icon, name, action and a green tick; 4) the answer streams word by word with a blinking block cursor, then a \"Done in 6.2s · 4 models · 112 agents · 4 tools\" line. Runs are data objects. Put the thread in a role=\"log\" region, hide the side panes on smaller screens and show results instantly under prefers-reduced-motion."},
{id:"liquid-tabs",uses:["L49"],t:"Liquid tab indicator",cat:"Navigation",tone:"light",added:"2026-10",d:"Pill and underline tabs whose highlight stretches toward the new tab like a drop of liquid, then catches up.",how:"The indicator's left and right edges are two separate springs. The leading edge is stiffer than the trailing one, so the pill stretches in the direction of travel and squashes slightly while it does. Real ARIA tabs with roving focus.",use:"Product sections, settings, pricing periods, any tab bar that should feel alive.",p:"Build liquid tabs in one HTML file, no libraries. A pill tab bar (role=tablist, four tabs with role=tab and matching tabpanels) has an absolutely positioned dark pill behind the active tab. Animate the pill's left and right edges as two independent damped springs: the edge moving in the direction of travel uses stiffness 0.2, the trailing edge 0.07, both with damping 0.72, so the pill stretches and then catches up; scale its height down a little while stretched. Arrow keys, Home and End move between tabs with roving tabindex; panels fade up 10px on change. Add a second underline-style tab bar using the same spring logic with a 3px rule. Under prefers-reduced-motion jump straight to the target."},
{id:"text-scramble",uses:["L05"],t:"Text scramble headline",cat:"Type",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"A big headline that decodes itself: letters churn through random glyphs and settle left to right, then cycle to the next phrase.",how:"Each character gets a random start and end frame. Before its start it shows the old letter, between start and end a random glyph that changes now and then, after its end the new letter. Screen readers get the real phrase in a hidden span.",use:"Agency and studio heroes, product launches, tech and security brands, nav links with personality.",p:"Build a text scramble headline in one HTML file, no libraries. Cycle through four phrases in a huge bold headline. To change phrase, give every character a random start frame (0–20 plus 0.6 per index) and end frame (start + 6–30); each animation frame render: old letter before start, a random glyph from \"!<>-_\\/[]{}=+*^?#A–Z0–9\" (in a monospace accent colour, re-rolled with 28% chance per frame) between start and end, the new letter after end. Hold each phrase 3.4s, with Next and Pause buttons. Put the real phrase in a visually hidden span and mark the animated span aria-hidden. Nav links scramble briefly on hover and focus. Under prefers-reduced-motion swap text instantly and stop cycling."},
{id:"scroll-scrub",uses:["L19","L08"],t:"Scroll-scrubbed product reveal",cat:"Scroll",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"A device tips back, splits into its layers and powers on, all driven by how far you have scrolled.",how:"A tall section holds a sticky stage. Scroll progress from 0 to 1 is split into phases with eased segments: tilt, explode the four layers along Z, label them, then light the screen. Captions and a step rail change with the phase.",use:"Hardware and app launches, explaining how a product is built, annual reports with one hero object.",p:"Build a scroll-scrubbed product reveal in one HTML file, no libraries. A 460vh section contains a 100vh sticky stage: captions on the left, a CSS 3D device on the right made of four stacked layers (shell, logic board drawn with CSS grid lines, screen image, glass). Compute progress p = −sectionTop / (sectionHeight − viewportHeight). Use eased segments: 0.06–0.3 tilts the device to rotateX(56deg) rotateZ(−34deg); 0.3–0.55 separates layers along translateZ up to 95px each and fades in a label for each layer; 0.72–0.9 brightens the screen; 0.8–0.97 brings it back together. Show four captions, one per quarter, plus a dot rail of buttons that scroll to each step and a progress bar. On phones stack the stage above the captions. Under prefers-reduced-motion show the exploded state statically with all captions listed."},
{id:"spotlight-grid",uses:["L10"],t:"Cursor spotlight grid",cat:"Cards",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"One soft light follows the pointer across a bento grid, catching each card's border and surface as it passes.",how:"Every card has a 1px gradient border made from its own background, centred on the pointer's position relative to that card. One shared, eased pointer drives all of them, so the light crosses gaps between cards. Focus moves the light by keyboard.",use:"SaaS feature grids, pricing tiers, dashboards, any dark product page that needs depth.",p:"Build a cursor spotlight grid in one HTML file, no libraries. Seven feature cards (two spanning two columns) in a 3-column grid on a near-black background. Each card is a link with 1px padding whose background is radial-gradient(420px circle at var(--lx) var(--ly), indigo 75%, faint, border colour), with an inner panel that has its own softer radial glow. On every animation frame, ease a shared light position toward the pointer (factor 0.14) and set --lx/--ly on each card relative to its own rect. When idle for 4s, drift the light slowly in a Lissajous path; when a card gets keyboard focus, move the light to it. Two columns under 820px, one under 540px. Under prefers-reduced-motion skip easing and the idle drift."},
{id:"morph-icons",uses:["L49","L15"],t:"Morphing icon toggles",cat:"Controls",tone:"light",added:"2026-10",d:"Play to pause, menu to close, plus to check, sound to mute, expand to collapse: icons that reshape on a spring instead of swapping.",how:"Each pair of SVG paths shares the same commands with different numbers. A tiny interpolator reads the numbers of both paths and springs between them each frame. The buttons are toggles with aria-pressed; the heart uses a CSS pop and a spark burst.",use:"Media players, navigation, list actions, likes and favourites, settings.",p:"Build six morphing icon toggles in one HTML file, no libraries. Each is a round button with aria-pressed and an SVG path. Write each icon pair with identical path commands so only numbers differ (play as two quads ↔ pause as two rects; three lines ↔ an X with a collapsed middle line; plus ↔ check; speaker waves ↔ X using quadratic curves; expand corners ↔ collapse corners). On toggle, interpolate every number with a spring (stiffness 0.16, damping 0.7) and rebuild the d attribute each frame. The sixth is a heart that fills, pops with a keyframe scale and throws ten sparks. Show a caption under each that reflects the state. Under prefers-reduced-motion set the end path immediately and skip sparks."},
{id:"sticker-physics",uses:["L30","L49"],t:"Draggable physics stickers",cat:"Cards",tone:"light",added:"2026-10",d:"A desk of stickers you can grab and throw. They spin, bounce off the edges and knock each other aside.",how:"Each sticker is a body with position, velocity and spin. Dragging records pointer velocity, so letting go throws it. Every frame applies friction, wall bounces and simple circle collisions. Keyboard users can nudge with arrows and toss with Enter.",use:"Playful about pages, event sites, merch and swag stores, portfolio easter eggs.",p:"Build a physics sticker board in one HTML file, no libraries. Eight stickers (round badges with text, pills, a star SVG, two photo stickers with white borders) on a dotted paper background. Each has x, y, vx, vy, angle and spin. Pointer down captures a sticker and lifts it (scale 1.08, bigger shadow); moving updates position and measures velocity; release throws it with that velocity and spin. Each frame: friction 0.975, spin damping 0.96, walls bounce at 0.7 and add spin, overlapping circles push apart and exchange impulse. Stickers are focusable role=button with labels: arrow keys move them (Shift for bigger steps), Enter tosses. Add Toss all and Tidy up buttons. Under prefers-reduced-motion, no momentum: stickers stay where they are dropped."},
{id:"velocity-marquee",uses:["L11"],t:"Velocity marquee",cat:"Type",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"Two endless bands of huge type that drift, then speed up, reverse and lean into the direction you scroll.",how:"Each band repeats its words four times and wraps its offset by one copy's width. Scroll and drag add to a velocity that decays each frame; the bands move at base speed plus that velocity and skew by a smoothed amount.",use:"Agency service lists, festival line-ups, fashion and sport landing pages, section dividers.",p:"Build a velocity-reactive marquee in one HTML file, no libraries. Two full-width bands of uppercase type (one filled, one outlined with -webkit-text-stroke) move in opposite directions. Duplicate each band's content four times and wrap the translateX offset by one copy's width so it never ends. Keep a velocity value: window scroll adds deltaY × 0.12, horizontal drag on a band adds to it, it decays by 0.92 per frame and clamps to ±60. Each band moves at 1.1px/frame plus |velocity| × 0.9, reverses direction when scrolling up, and is skewed by a smoothed velocity. Hide the bands from screen readers and provide the service list as hidden text. Add a Pause button (moving content longer than five seconds must be pausable). Under prefers-reduced-motion start paused."},
{id:"compare-slider",uses:["L04"],t:"Image comparison slider",cat:"Galleries",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"Before and after on one photo, split by a handle you can drag, tap, or move with the keyboard. Switches to a top-and-bottom split.",how:"Two copies of the same image are stacked; the top one is clipped with clip-path inset() at the handle position. The handle is a real role=slider with arrow, Page and Home/End keys, and labels fade as the handle reaches them.",use:"Retouching and colour grading, renovation and landscaping, product upgrades, data before and after.",p:"Build an image comparison slider in one HTML file, no libraries. Stack two copies of one photo: the bottom one filtered to look raw (grayscale, low contrast, slight sepia), the top one in full colour clipped with clip-path: inset(0 0 0 var(--p)). A 2px white divider with a round knob sits at --p. Pointer down anywhere glides the divider there with easing; dragging follows directly. The divider is role=slider with aria-valuenow and aria-valuetext (\"62% graded\"), arrow keys move 2%, Page Up/Down 10%, Home/End to the ends. Corner labels fade when the divider nears them. A segmented control switches to a vertical split. On load the divider sways once to show it moves. Under prefers-reduced-motion skip the intro and the easing."},
{id:"toast-stack",uses:["L39","L49"],t:"Stacked toasts with swipe",cat:"Feedback",tone:"light",added:"2026-10",d:"Notifications that collapse into a neat deck, fan out when you hover or focus them, and swipe away sideways.",how:"Toasts are absolutely positioned from the bottom. Collapsed, they share the front toast's height and peek out 14px each, slightly smaller. Expanded, each sits at its natural height. Timers pause while the stack is open; a drag past 110px or a fast flick dismisses.",use:"Any app: saves, uploads, undo actions, errors, chat messages.",p:"Build stacked toast notifications in one HTML file, no libraries. Buttons fire four kinds of toast (success, info, undo action, error), each with an icon, title, message, optional action button, close button and a thin progress bar. Toasts live in a bottom-right region with aria-live=polite. Collapsed: newest in front, older ones offset up 14px and scaled 5% smaller each, all clipped to the front toast's height, only three visible, back ones inert. Hovering or focusing the region expands them into a column using their natural heights. Each toast auto-dismisses after 5.2s, paused while expanded. Dragging right moves it with rubber-band resistance to the left; past 110px or a fast flick it flies out. Escape dismisses a focused toast and moves focus to the next. Keep at most five."},
{id:"pull-refresh",uses:["L15","L49"],t:"Elastic pull-to-refresh",cat:"Feedback",tone:"light",added:"2026-10",d:"A phone inbox you can pull down from the top. It stretches like rubber, a ring fills, and past the line it refreshes and drops in a new message.",how:"When the feed is scrolled to the top, a downward drag is passed through an exponential rubber-band curve. A ring's stroke-dashoffset tracks progress to the threshold; release past it spins the ring, waits, prepends an item and eases back.",use:"Mobile web apps, feeds, inboxes, dashboards that load fresh data.",p:"Build an elastic pull-to-refresh in one HTML file, no libraries, inside a phone frame. When the feed's scrollTop is 0, a downward pointer or touch drag sets the list's translateY to max × (1 − e^(−d / max / 1.1)) with max 150px. A ring (two SVG circles) appears above the list; its progress arc fills as the pull reaches the 90px threshold, and the label switches from \"Pull to refresh\" to \"Release to refresh\". Releasing past the threshold settles at 64px, spins the ring for 1.3s, prepends a new message with a slide-in animation and eases back to 0 (cubic ease-out, 380ms). Use touch events with preventDefault for touch, pointer events for mouse. Add a visible Refresh button and an R shortcut on the focused feed, and a role=status line saying what changed."},
{id:"flip-cards",uses:["L08","L49"],t:"3D flip cards",cat:"Cards",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"Image cards that turn over in 3D to show details, flipping away from wherever you pressed: left, right, top or bottom.",how:"Each card is a button with a front and a back face, backface-visibility hidden. The click position picks the axis and direction; a springy cubic-bezier overshoots slightly. aria-pressed and aria-hidden keep the two sides honest for screen readers.",use:"Team bios, materials and swatches, product specs, menus, flashcards.",p:"Build 3D flip cards in one HTML file with CSS 3D transforms and a little JS. Four tall image cards in a grid with perspective 1400px. Each card is a button containing a front face (image, numeral, serif title) and a back face (title, description, small spec list) with backface-visibility: hidden. On click, compare the click position to the card centre: mostly vertical offset flips around X, mostly horizontal flips around Y, in the direction away from the press. Use an 800ms cubic-bezier(.3,1.35,.45,1) transition. Toggle aria-pressed and aria-hidden on the back. Keyboard Enter flips around Y. Add Flip all and Reset buttons that stagger by 110ms. Under prefers-reduced-motion change sides without animating."},
{id:"split-flap",uses:["L27"],t:"Split-flap departure board",cat:"Type",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"An airport board where every character clacks through the alphabet to its new letter, row by row, every few seconds.",how:"Each cell has four halves: static top and bottom, plus a top flap that falls and a bottom flap that rises, animated with the Web Animations API. To change letter, a cell steps through the character set one flip at a time. A hidden table carries the real data.",use:"Travel and events, launch countdowns, scoreboards, retro-industrial brands, live status pages.",p:"Build a split-flap departure board in one HTML file, no libraries. Six rows with columns time (5), destination (10), gate (3) and status (7), each a row of cells. A cell has four half-height layers with overflow hidden: static upper (next char), static lower (current char), an upper flap showing the current char that rotates X from 0 to −90° around its bottom edge, then a lower flap showing the next char that rotates from 90° to 0 around its top edge. To change a cell, step through ' A–Z0–9:.-' at most ten flips (55ms each, 110ms for the last), staggered by row and column. Colour rows by status. Every 6s scroll to the next flight, with Update and Pause buttons. Mark the board aria-hidden and keep a visually hidden table with the same data. Under 700px hide time and gate. Under prefers-reduced-motion set characters instantly and start paused."},
{id:"radial-menu",uses:["L12","L49"],t:"Radial menu",cat:"Navigation",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"A round button that blooms into a ring of six actions. Walk it with arrow keys, or press, hold, slide and release like a game menu.",how:"Items sit on a circle with cos/sin offsets and spring out with staggered delays. A conic-gradient sector highlights the item under the pointer angle while you hold. It is a proper menu button: aria-expanded, role=menu, focus moves into the ring and Escape returns it.",use:"Creative tools, maps and canvases, mobile quick actions, games.",p:"Build a radial menu in one HTML file, no libraries. A 76px round hub button (aria-haspopup=menu, aria-expanded) opens six 58px menu items placed on a circle of radius 118px (100px on phones) using translate(cos·R, sin·R), starting at the top. They spring out with cubic-bezier(.2,1.4,.4,1) and 30ms stagger; the plus icon rotates 135°. A dashed ring and a conic-gradient 60° sector highlight the hovered item, and its name shows above the ring. Keyboard: opening with Space or Enter focuses the first item, arrows move around the ring, Escape closes and refocuses the hub. Press and hold the hub for 220ms to open, slide toward an item by angle and release to choose it. Report the choice in a role=status line. Under prefers-reduced-motion only fade."},
{id:"odometer",uses:["L27"],t:"Odometer counter",cat:"Type",tone:"light",added:"2026-10",d:"Big numbers that roll like a mechanical counter. Carries spin every wheel they touch, so you feel how far it went.",how:"Each digit is a window onto a strip of 0–9 repeated three times. Moving from one digit to the next translates the strip by the difference; a change of ten or more in that place adds a full turn. Separators stay still, and a status line announces the new total.",use:"Fundraising totals, live counters, pricing calculators, stats sections, dashboards.",p:"Build an odometer counter in one HTML file, no libraries. Format the number with thousands separators; each digit is an overflow-hidden 1em window containing a vertical strip of 30 digits (0–9 three times), resting at the middle copy. To change value, start each strip at 10 + oldDigit and transition translateY to 10 + oldDigit + delta em, where delta is the forward (or backward) distance to the new digit, plus a full turn when the change in that place is ten or more. Use 1.2s cubic-bezier(.2,.9,.15,1) with a 40ms stagger per digit; rebuild the strips when the digit count changes. Show a huge total with a currency sign, buttons (+1, +250, big donation), a number input with Set, and three smaller stat counters. Hide the strips from screen readers and announce totals in a role=status element. Under prefers-reduced-motion no transition."},
{id:"gooey-nav",uses:["L48","L49"],t:"Gooey blob navigation",cat:"Navigation",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"A nav bar whose highlight is liquid: switching sections pinches off droplets that race ahead and merge again.",how:"The highlight and three droplets are springs drawn in a layer filtered by an SVG goo filter (blur, then a sharp alpha threshold), so nearby shapes melt together. Labels sit above the filtered layer and stay crisp. Hovering pulls a bead toward the hovered link.",use:"Portfolio and studio sites, playful product navs, mobile tab bars.",p:"Build gooey navigation in one HTML file, no libraries. A pill nav with five links; behind them, a layer with filter: url(#goo) where the SVG filter is feGaussianBlur stdDeviation 7 followed by feColorMatrix alpha row \"0 0 0 22 −9\" and feComposite atop. In that layer draw the main blob (the active link's width) and three small circular droplets, each a spring (x and width) with different stiffness. On change, droplets start at the old link and spring toward the new one faster than the main blob, then shrink back into it after 420ms, so the shape stretches and splits. Hovering another link pulls one droplet toward it. Labels sit above the filter layer; the active label uses aria-current=page. Update a big serif headline for the section. Under prefers-reduced-motion move the blob instantly."},
{id:"terminal-type",uses:["L05"],t:"Typewriter terminal",cat:"Type",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"A terminal that types out an install and boot sequence with human rhythm and a progress bar, then lets you type your own commands.",how:"The script is data: commands, output lines, waits and progress bars. Commands type one character at a time with jittered delays. The output is a role=log that stays quiet during the scripted part and speaks for your own commands.",use:"Developer tools, CLIs and SDKs, API products, technical hiring pages.",p:"Build a typewriter terminal in one HTML file, no libraries. A window with traffic-light dots, a title and a Replay button. Play a script array of steps: cmd (type character by character at 28–98ms, slower on spaces, with a block cursor), out (print an HTML line with green ticks, yellow highlights and blue links), wait, and bar (an ASCII progress bar filling 24 blocks with a percentage). After the script, show a prompt. Below, a form with an input: Enter runs help, ls, whoami, date, clear or replay, and prints \"command not found\" otherwise, escaping user text. The output is role=log with aria-live off while the script plays and polite afterwards. The cursor blinks for a few seconds only. Under prefers-reduced-motion print everything instantly."},
{id:"text-cylinder",uses:["L08","L49"],t:"Rotating 3D word drum",cat:"Type",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"\"We design ___\": twelve words on the faces of a 3D cylinder that turns to the next one, or spins under your finger.",how:"Words are placed with rotateX(−i·30°) translateZ(r), where r comes from the line height and the word count. A spring eases the drum to the chosen face; a vertical mask fades the faces above and below. It is a spinbutton whose value text is the current word.",use:"Agency heroes, \"we make…\" statements, product taglines, feature lists in a small space.",p:"Build a 3D word drum headline in one HTML file, no libraries. The headline reads \"We design\" followed by a drum: twelve words on the faces of a cylinder around the X axis. Radius r = (lineHeight / 2) / tan(π / 12); word i gets rotateX(−i·30deg) translateZ(r) and the cylinder gets translateZ(−r) rotateX(angle). Show only faces within 65° and mask the drum with a vertical gradient so neighbours fade. A spring (0.08 stiffness, 0.72 damping) eases the angle to the target word. Drag vertically to turn, wheel and arrow keys step, plus up/down buttons. Auto-advance every 2.2s with a Pause button. The drum is role=spinbutton with aria-valuetext set to the word; announce changes politely. Under prefers-reduced-motion snap and start paused."},
{id:"depth-hero",uses:["L01"],t:"Parallax depth hero",cat:"Scroll",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"A sunset mountain range in seven layers that slide at different speeds as you scroll and move the pointer. The title sinks behind the near hills.",how:"Each layer carries a depth value. Scroll moves far layers down more slowly than the page, pointer movement shifts them sideways in proportion to depth, and the headline sits between the ridges in the stacking order. SVG birds flap along.",use:"Travel, outdoor and hospitality brands, event heroes, storytelling intros.",p:"Build a parallax depth hero in one HTML file, no libraries. A 100svh header with a sunset gradient sky, CSS stars, a glowing sun, and four SVG mountain ridges (polyline paths, preserveAspectRatio none) in darkening purples, plus a mist band. Place a huge bold headline between the second and third ridges in DOM order. Give every layer data-d from 0.05 (far) to 1 (near). Each frame, ease the pointer position and set translate3d(−px·d·60px, scrollY·(1−d)·0.6 − py·d·24px). Animate a few SVG birds flapping with quadratic curves. Follow with a content section. Under prefers-reduced-motion leave every layer still."},
{id:"glitch-images",uses:["L48"],t:"Glitch hover on images",cat:"Galleries",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"A gallery where each frame breaks up when you hover or focus it: the red channel slips, slices jump, scanlines roll in, then it heals.",how:"Two SVG colour-matrix filters split the image into red and green-blue copies. On hover the base shows green-blue, a red-only copy screens over it with a jittered offset, and a full-colour copy shows random clipped slices shifted sideways.",use:"Music, gaming and fashion sites, tech and cyber brands, event line-ups.",p:"Build a glitch-hover image grid in one HTML file, no libraries. Define two SVG filters: one keeps only the red channel, one keeps green and blue. Each tile stacks three copies of an image: base, a red-only copy with mix-blend-mode: screen, and a full-colour slice copy hidden with clip-path. On hover or focus, filter the base to green-blue, show the red copy, and every 40–100ms jitter the red copy's translate by up to 12px, and with 55% chance show a random horizontal slice of the slice copy (clip-path: inset(a% 0 b% 0)) shifted up to 20px. Add scanlines with a repeating gradient. On leave, decay over 300ms. Four columns, two on phones; tiles are links with a caption. Under prefers-reduced-motion show a calm grayscale change instead."},
{id:"horizontal-story",uses:["L03"],t:"Horizontal scroll story",cat:"Scroll",tone:"light",added:"2026-10",d:"Scroll down and the story moves sideways through five chapters, with photographs drifting inside their frames and a chapter bar that keeps up.",how:"The section's height is set to the strip's overflow width plus one screen. A sticky viewport translates the strip by how far the section has scrolled. Images get a counter-offset based on distance from the centre; chapter buttons jump to each panel.",use:"Editorial features, case studies, timelines, product tours, annual reports.",p:"Build a horizontal scroll story in one HTML file, no libraries. A section contains a 100svh sticky viewport with a top bar (title and chapter buttons), a progress bar and a flex strip of panels: an intro panel with a huge serif title, then four chapter panels each with text and a framed photo. Set the section height to strip.scrollWidth − innerWidth + innerHeight. On scroll, translate the strip by clamp(scrollY − sectionTop), update progress and mark the current chapter with aria-current=step. Shift each image inside its frame by −12% × its distance from the viewport centre. Chapter buttons scroll to the matching panel. On phones panels stack text above image. Under prefers-reduced-motion drop the pinning and show panels vertically."},
{id:"flip-masonry",uses:["L13"],t:"Masonry with FLIP filtering",cat:"Galleries",tone:"light",added:"2026-10",d:"A masonry archive where filtering and shuffling glide every picture to its new spot instead of jumping.",how:"A small JS masonry places items absolutely into the shortest column. When the filter or order changes, it recalculates and transitions transforms, so items fly to their new positions while hidden ones shrink and fade. DOM order follows visual order for keyboard users.",use:"Portfolios, archives, product catalogues, image libraries, resource hubs.",p:"Build a filterable masonry gallery in one HTML file, no libraries. Sixteen images with different aspect ratios and tags. Lay them out with JS: column count 4 (3 under 900px, 2 under 520px), each item placed absolutely in the shortest column with translate(x,y); the wall's height animates to the tallest column. Filter chips (All, Nature, Craft, Sky, Sea) with aria-pressed, and a Shuffle button. On change, transition transform (600ms cubic-bezier(.2,.8,.2,1)) and opacity; hidden items scale to 0.6, fade and become visibility hidden and aria-hidden; newly shown ones fade in after 150ms. Reorder the DOM to match visual order before animating. A role=status line reports the count. Re-layout on resize with ResizeObserver. Under prefers-reduced-motion move instantly."},
{id:"ripple-buttons",uses:["L04"],t:"Ripple buttons",cat:"Buttons",tone:"light",added:"2026-10",d:"Six button styles where ink spreads from exactly where you pressed, waits while you hold, and fades when you let go.",how:"On pointer down a circle sized to reach the farthest corner is added at the press point and scaled up with the Web Animations API. It fades only after both the grow has finished and the pointer is released. Keyboard presses ripple from the centre.",use:"Any interface: primary actions, icon buttons, switches, mobile-first apps.",p:"Build a set of ripple buttons in one HTML file, no libraries. Six variants: solid, outline, an ink-fill button whose label uses mix-blend-mode: difference so it stays readable as black ink fills white, a round icon button, a gradient pill, and a role=switch toggle. On pointerdown, add a span at the press point with diameter 2 × the distance to the farthest corner, and animate scale 0→1 over 520ms (900ms for the ink fill). Fade it out over 420ms only after the grow finishes and the pointer is released or leaves. Enter and Space ripple from the centre. Buttons have overflow hidden and isolation isolate, scale to 0.97 while pressed, and show clear focus rings. Under prefers-reduced-motion show a brief flat flash instead."},
{id:"dot-clock",uses:["L47","L27"],t:"Dot-matrix clock",cat:"Canvas",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"A glowing LED clock drawn in 5×7 dots. When a digit changes its dots fly to their new places, and your pointer pushes them around.",how:"Each character position owns a pool of dots. When its character changes, its dots get new targets from a 5×7 font, in shuffled order so they cross paths. Every frame, dots spring toward their targets and are pushed away from the pointer. A hidden line gives the time to screen readers.",use:"Event countdowns, live status pages, retro-tech and audio brands, kiosk screens.",p:"Build a dot-matrix clock on a canvas in one HTML file, no libraries. Define a 5×7 bitmap font for 0–9, colon, A, P and M. Render HH:MM:SS centred, with a faint grid of unlit dots behind. Keep a pool of dots per character position; when that character changes, assign its dots to the new lit cells in random order (creating dots as needed, fading out extras). Each frame, spring dots toward their targets (0.07 stiffness, 0.78 damping), repel them within four cells of the pointer, and draw them with an LED colour and glow. Buttons toggle 24-hour and seconds and pick the LED colour; Enter on the focused canvas scatters the dots. Show the date above. Keep a visually hidden element with the time. Under prefers-reduced-motion place dots directly."},
{id:"synth-bars",uses:["L16"],t:"Audio-reactive bars",cat:"Canvas",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"A small synth made with Web Audio plays a loop and an analyser draws it as bars or a ring. No audio files, no microphone, silent until you press play.",how:"A lookahead scheduler queues kick, hats, a filtered saw bass and a square arpeggio on the audio clock. The master gain feeds an AnalyserNode whose frequency data is smoothed into 64 log-spaced bars. Before play, a simulated spectrum keeps the visual alive.",use:"Music, podcast and audio products, events and festivals, creative coding showcases.",p:"Build an audio-reactive visualiser in one HTML file using only the Web Audio API and canvas. On the first Play click create an AudioContext, a master gain and an AnalyserNode (fftSize 256). Schedule sixteenth notes with a 25ms timer and 120ms lookahead: kick on beats (sine 140→42Hz), noise hi-hats on offbeats, a lowpassed sawtooth bass and a quiet square arpeggio. Read getByteFrequencyData every frame, map 64 bars on a log-ish scale (32 on phones) and smooth them. Draw rounded gradient bars, a radial ring of strokes, or both. Add tempo and volume sliders and a view select. Before audio starts, draw a simulated spectrum and say so in a role=status line. Under prefers-reduced-motion freeze the simulation."},
{id:"kinetic-grid",uses:["L16","L10"],t:"Kinetic needle grid",cat:"Canvas",tone:"light",added:"2026-10",d:"A field of needles like iron filings that turn to face your pointer, stretch as you get close and ripple when you click.",how:"Needles sit on a regular grid. Each eases its angle toward the pointer (or perpendicular, or away) by the shortest turn, and its length grows with closeness. Clicks spawn expanding rings that kick nearby needles and paint them red.",use:"Hero backgrounds, engineering and science brands, interactive 404 pages, loading states.",p:"Build a kinetic needle field on a full-screen canvas in one HTML file, no libraries. Place needles every 30px (24px on phones). Each frame, each needle's target angle is atan2 toward an eased pointer position; turn by the shortest signed difference × 0.2. Length is gap × 0.42 × max(0.18, 1 − distance / (diagonal × 0.45)); line width and opacity follow length; needles near the pointer turn red. Clicks add a ring that expands 9px per frame and boosts needles within 40px of its edge. Mode buttons switch between face, orbit (+90°) and repel (+180°). When idle, the target orbits slowly. The canvas is focusable: arrow keys move the target, Enter sends a ripple. Under prefers-reduced-motion snap instead of easing and stop the idle orbit."},
{id:"stack-cards",uses:["L21"],t:"Sticky stacking cards",cat:"Scroll",tone:"light",added:"2026-10",d:"Process steps that pin near the top while the next one slides over, and the ones underneath shrink back and darken like a dealt deck.",how:"Each card is position: sticky with a top offset that grows by index, so they pile up with an edge showing. On scroll, each card measures how far the cards after it have arrived and scales down and dims by that amount.",use:"Process and method sections, feature tours, pricing stories, onboarding steps.",p:"Build sticky stacking cards in one HTML file, no libraries. Five full-width cards with their own background colour, step number, serif title, text and photo. Each is position: sticky with top: calc(10vh + index × 22px) and a gap between them so the next card scrolls up over the previous one. On scroll, for each card sum how far each later card has travelled toward it (clamped 0–1 over 70% of the viewport, capped at 3) and set scale(1 − sum × 0.045) with transform-origin top, plus a black overlay at opacity sum × 0.16. On phones stack the text above a shorter image. Under prefers-reduced-motion keep the sticky stack but no scaling or dimming."},
{id:"lens-zoom",uses:["L10"],t:"Magnifier lens",cat:"Galleries",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"A round lens that follows your pointer over a photo at 2.5× zoom. Wheel or keys change the zoom, and on touch it floats above your finger.",how:"The lens is a circle whose background is the same image scaled by the zoom factor and offset so the point under the pointer sits in the middle. An eased position keeps it smooth. On touch the lens is lifted three quarters of its size above the finger.",use:"Product detail pages, art and craft close-ups, maps and diagrams, photography portfolios.",p:"Build a magnifier lens in one HTML file, no libraries. A photo in a rounded frame; a 190px circular lens (140px on phones) with a white ring and shadow follows the pointer with easing. Its background-image is the photo, background-size = frame size × zoom, background-position = −(point × zoom − lens / 2). Zoom 1.5–5×, default 2.5×, shown in a small badge; the wheel changes it while the lens is shown, plus and minus buttons too. On touch, draw the lens 0.75 × its size above the finger. The frame is focusable: focus shows the lens, arrow keys move it (Shift for bigger steps), + and − zoom, Escape hides it. Thumbnails switch the picture. Under prefers-reduced-motion follow without easing."},
{id:"hold-button",uses:["L15","L46"],t:"Press-and-hold confirm",cat:"Buttons",tone:"dark",added:"2026-10",d:"A destructive button you have to hold down. It fills while you press, drains if you let go early, and turns into a green tick with confetti when done.",how:"A progress value rises while the pointer or Space/Enter is held and drains quickly when released. Reaching 1 completes the action. A quick tap is caught and explained with a small shake instead of doing nothing silently. Undo puts everything back.",use:"Deleting, cancelling subscriptions, publishing, payments, anything that should not happen by accident.",p:"Build a press-and-hold confirm button in one HTML file, no libraries. A wide pill button with a red outline and a fill layer scaled on X by a progress value. While the pointer is down (with pointer capture) or Space/Enter is held (ignore key repeat), progress rises to 1 over 1.3s; when released it drains in 350ms. At 1, mark done: switch to a green fill and a tick icon labelled Deleted, dim the file card, burst 18 confetti pieces, and show an Undo button. A release under 250ms shows \"Hold the button down to delete\" and shakes the button. All messages go in a role=status line referenced by aria-describedby. Under prefers-reduced-motion skip the shake and confetti but keep the fill."},
{id:"rubber-slider",uses:["L49"],t:"Elastic rubber-band slider",cat:"Controls",tone:"light",added:"2026-10",d:"Sliders with a little give: drag past either end and the track stretches like rubber, then snaps back. The value bubble leans with your speed.",how:"Inside the range the value follows the pointer. Past the ends, the overshoot is the square root of the distance, so it resists more the further you pull; the rail scales from the far end and thins. A spring returns it on release. Built as role=slider with full keyboard support.",use:"Volume and media controls, thermostats and smart home, filters, playful settings screens.",p:"Build an elastic slider in one HTML file, no libraries. A custom role=slider with aria-valuemin/max/now/valuetext, a rail with a fill, ticks and a round thumb carrying a value bubble. Dragging inside the track sets the value by position and step. Dragging beyond either end sets an overshoot of 5.5 × √distance: the rail scales on X by 1 + overshoot / width from the opposite end (transform-origin), thins on Y, and the thumb follows. On release, spring the overshoot back to 0 (0.2 stiffness, 0.6 damping). The shown value springs toward the real value; the bubble rotates with pointer velocity. Keys: arrows by one step, Page Up/Down by a tenth, Home and End; pressing past an end gives a small overshoot kick. Make two: a volume and a thermostat with a gradient fill. Under prefers-reduced-motion skip the springs."},
{id:"reorder-list",uses:["L13"],t:"Drag to reorder list",cat:"Lists",tone:"light",added:"2026-10",d:"A to-do list you can reorder by dragging a handle or entirely by keyboard, with rows that slide out of the way and spoken updates.",how:"While dragging, the row follows the pointer and swaps with a neighbour when it crosses that neighbour's midpoint; FLIP animates the others into place. On a keyboard, Space picks up, arrows move, Space drops, Escape cancels, and an assertive live region narrates each step.",use:"Task lists, playlists, priorities, settings order, form builders.",p:"Build an accessible reorderable list in one HTML file, no libraries. Six rows, each with a drag-handle button (labelled \"Move: title\"), a position number, title, duration and tag. Pointer drag on the handle lifts the row (shadow, scale 1.02) and follows the pointer; when its midpoint passes a neighbour's midpoint, swap them in the DOM and animate the others with FLIP (measure, mutate, invert with translateY, play). Keyboard: Space or Enter on a handle picks the row up, Up and Down move it, Space drops, Escape returns it to where it started, leaving focus clears the pick-up. Announce every step in an aria-live=assertive element (\"Picked up…\", \"Position 3 of 6\", \"Dropped…\"). Renumber after each move. Under prefers-reduced-motion skip the FLIP animation."},
{id:"confetti-button",uses:["L16"],t:"Confetti button",cat:"Buttons",tone:"dark",added:"2026-11",d:"A button that throws a burst of paper confetti from its own centre each time you press it.",how:"Each press adds ninety small rectangles to one full-screen canvas, launched upward in a fan from the button with random speed and spin. Gravity and drag act every frame, each piece fades as its life runs out, and the loop stops itself when none are left.",use:"Task done, order placed, goal reached, sign-up complete.",p:"Build a confetti button in one HTML file, no libraries. A centred pill button over a fixed full-screen canvas with pointer-events none. On click, spawn 90 particles at the button centre, aimed upward within about 60 degrees either side, speed 6 to 16, each a small rotating rectangle in one of six colours. Every frame apply gravity and air drag, rotate, reduce life, and draw; stop the animation loop when the array is empty. Scale the canvas for device pixel ratio up to 2. Announce the count of presses in a role=status line. Under prefers-reduced-motion skip the particles and only update the status text."},
{id:"like-burst",uses:["L27","L49"],t:"Like button with a burst",cat:"Buttons",tone:"light",added:"2026-11",d:"A heart that pops, sends out a ring and eight dots, and rolls its count up by one.",how:"The button is a real toggle with aria-pressed. Pressing it scales the heart with a spring curve, expands a ring behind it and flies eight absolutely placed dots outward along angles set as CSS variables. The count is two stacked numbers in a clipped box that slides up one line.",use:"Likes, saves, favourites, upvotes, reactions.",p:"Build a like button in one HTML file, no libraries. A button with aria-pressed, an SVG heart, a visually hidden label and a count. On press: fill the heart and scale it 0.6 to 1.25 to 1 with a springy easing, expand and fade a circular ring, and send eight small coloured dots outward at 45 degree steps using per-dot CSS variables for angle and colour. The count shows two numbers stacked in an overflow-hidden box one line tall; translate it up one line when liked. Unpressing reverses without the burst. Update the accessible label with the new count. Under prefers-reduced-motion change colour and number only."},
{id:"magnetic-dock",uses:["L09"],t:"Magnifying dock",cat:"Navigation",tone:"light",added:"2026-11",d:"A row of app icons that swell under the pointer, with neighbours growing a little less, like a desktop dock.",how:"Each icon gets a target scale from its distance to the pointer, falling off over about 130 pixels with a squared curve. Current scales ease toward targets every frame, and icons grow from their bottom edge so the row lifts rather than shifts. Focus moves with arrow keys and magnifies the focused icon the same way.",use:"App launchers, tool bars, tab bars, social link rows.",p:"Build a magnifying dock in one HTML file, no libraries. A rounded glass bar holding seven icon buttons in a list, each with an aria-label and a small name tooltip. On pointermove over the dock compute each icon centre distance from the pointer and set a target scale of 1 + 0.75 * max(0, 1 - d/130) squared; on leave set all targets to 1. In requestAnimationFrame ease current scale toward target by 20 percent per frame and apply it with transform-origin at the bottom; stop the loop when settled. Left and Right arrows move focus, and focus magnifies that icon. Under prefers-reduced-motion jump straight to the target."},
{id:"wave-text",uses:["L24","L49"],t:"Wave text",cat:"Type",tone:"dark",added:"2026-11",d:"A headline whose letters rise under the pointer and settle back like a plucked string.",how:"The word is split into one span per letter with the whole word kept as the accessible name. Each letter has a spring: its goal height is a bell curve of its distance from the pointer, and velocity and damping carry it there with a little overshoot. When the pointer rests, a slow travelling wave takes over.",use:"Hero headlines, playful logos, section titles, 404 pages.",p:"Build a wave headline in one HTML file, no libraries. Split an h1 into one aria-hidden span per letter and put the full text in aria-label. Track pointer x. Every frame, for each letter compute goal = 46 * exp(-((pointerX - letterCentre)/90)^2), then integrate a spring: v += (goal - y) * 0.14; v *= 0.78; y += v; apply translateY(-y) through a CSS variable and tint the letter by height. After about four seconds without movement run a gentle sine wave across the letters instead. Under prefers-reduced-motion leave the text still."},
{id:"word-rotator",uses:["L24"],t:"Rotating headline word",cat:"Type",tone:"light",added:"2026-11",d:"A sentence where one word keeps changing: the old word slides out as the next slides in and the gap eases to fit.",how:"All the words sit stacked in one inline box. The current one has a class that brings it to rest, the leaving one a class that sends it up and out, and the box width animates to the measured width of the new word so the rest of the sentence glides. A pause button stops the timer.",use:"Hero headlines, value propositions, service lists, taglines.",p:"Build a rotating-word headline in one HTML file, no libraries. An h1 with a fixed sentence and one inline-block slot holding five words absolutely stacked. Every 2.4 seconds give the next word class on (translateY 0, opacity 1), the previous class out (translateY -60 percent, opacity 0), and animate the slot width to the new word offsetWidth over 0.5 seconds. Only the visible word is exposed to assistive tech. Add a Pause button with aria-pressed. Re-measure after fonts load. Under prefers-reduced-motion do not auto-rotate; the button becomes Next word and swaps instantly."},
{id:"morph-blob",uses:["L15"],t:"Morphing blob",cat:"Canvas",tone:"dark",added:"2026-11",d:"A soft gradient shape that never stops changing and leans toward your pointer.",how:"Eight points sit around a circle, each with a radius driven by two sine waves on different clocks. A smooth closed curve is drawn through them every frame and written to one SVG path. Points near the pointer are pulled outward, with the pull easing in and out.",use:"Hero backdrops, avatar frames, image masks, empty states.",p:"Build a morphing blob in one HTML file, no libraries. An SVG with a linear gradient and one path. Each frame compute 8 points at equal angles with radius 78 + 12*sin(t/900 + i*1.7) + 8*sin(t/1400 + i*3.1); pull each point toward the pointer by an amount that falls with distance, scaled by an eased 0 to 1 pull value. Draw a closed smooth curve through the points using midpoints as anchors and the points as quadratic controls, and set it as the path d. Put a heading and line of copy over the shape. Under prefers-reduced-motion draw one frame and stop."},
{id:"loader-set",uses:["L46"],t:"Loader set",cat:"Feedback",tone:"light",added:"2026-11",d:"Six loading indicators in CSS only: ring, bouncing dots, equaliser, chasing squares, stretching arc and orbit.",how:"Every loader is a small box with a few child elements and one keyframe animation each; delays stagger the children. All take colour from one custom property and size from another, so a single rule restyles the set.",use:"Buttons, page loads, skeleton states, upload progress, inline waits.",p:"Build six CSS-only loaders in one HTML file, shown in a grid with a label under each, every one role=img with aria-label Loading. 1 Ring: a bordered circle with one coloured side, rotating. 2 Three dots bouncing with staggered delays. 3 Four bars scaling vertically like an equaliser. 4 Four squares that take turns moving around a 2 by 2 grid. 5 An SVG circle whose stroke-dasharray stretches and contracts while the whole rotates. 6 A dot orbiting a faint ring. Use one --c colour variable and one --s size variable. Under prefers-reduced-motion slow every animation to several seconds instead of removing it, so the state still reads as loading."},
{id:"progress-steps",uses:["L15","L12"],t:"Animated progress steps",cat:"Feedback",tone:"light",added:"2026-11",d:"A checkout stepper whose connecting line fills, dots turn into drawn ticks and the panel slides as you move on.",how:"The steps are an ordered list with the current one marked aria-current. Classes for done and now drive everything: the connector grows by scaling a pseudo-element, the tick draws with a dash offset, and the active dot pulses once. The panel below swaps content inside a polite live region.",use:"Checkouts, onboarding, sign-up flows, multi-page forms.",p:"Build an animated stepper in one HTML file, no libraries. An ordered list of four steps (Cart, Delivery, Payment, Done) with a numbered dot and label each, a content panel with aria-live=polite, and Back and Continue buttons. Completed steps get class done: the dot fills with the accent, the number fades and an SVG tick draws via stroke-dashoffset; the connector to the next step fills by scaleX from the left. The current step gets aria-current=step and a ring. Changing step replaces the panel content with a short slide and fade. Disable Back on the first step; on the last, Continue becomes Start again. Under prefers-reduced-motion remove the transitions."},
{id:"theme-switch",uses:["L20","L04"],t:"Day and night switch",cat:"Controls",tone:"light",added:"2026-11",d:"A switch where the sun rolls across and becomes a moon, while the page changes colour in a circle growing from the switch.",how:"It is a real role=switch button. The knob slides and a second circle cuts a crescent out of it; stars drop in and a cloud drifts away. The colour change runs as a view transition, with the new page revealed by a clip-path circle that expands from the switch centre.",use:"Dark mode toggles, settings pages, site headers.",p:"Build a day and night theme switch in one HTML file, no libraries. A button with role=switch and aria-checked. Inside: a round knob that slides right when on, a same-colour-as-track circle that moves over the knob to carve a crescent, three star dots that fall into place, and a cloud that slides out. Theme colours are custom properties switched by a data attribute on the root. On click, if document.startViewTransition exists, change the attribute inside it and animate ::view-transition-new(root) with clip-path from circle(0 at x y) to circle(R at x y), where x,y is the switch centre and R reaches the farthest corner. Otherwise, or under prefers-reduced-motion, switch instantly."},
{id:"holo-card",uses:["L08","L10"],t:"Holographic card",cat:"Cards",tone:"dark",added:"2026-11",d:"A membership card that tilts toward the pointer with a rainbow foil and glare that follow the light.",how:"Pointer position inside the card is turned into two rotation angles and a pair of percentages. The angles tilt the card in 3D; the percentages move a repeating rainbow gradient blended over the surface and a radial glare. Arrow keys nudge the same values so it works without a mouse.",use:"Membership cards, tickets, collectibles, pricing highlights, badges.",p:"Build a holographic card in one HTML file, no libraries. A focusable card inside a container with perspective. On pointermove compute nx, ny from 0 to 1 across the card and set CSS variables: rotateY (nx - 0.5) * 26deg, rotateX (0.5 - ny) * 22deg, and --mx, --my percentages. One pseudo-element holds a repeating linear rainbow gradient whose background-position follows --mx and --my, with mix-blend-mode color-dodge; another holds a radial white glare centred on the pointer. Fade both in on hover and ease back to flat on leave. Arrow keys shift the values in steps. Under prefers-reduced-motion keep the foil and glare but no tilt."},
{id:"line-draw",uses:["L15","L12"],t:"Line drawing illustration",cat:"Canvas",tone:"light",added:"2026-11",d:"A small landscape that draws itself stroke by stroke, then fills in its sun, with a button to draw it again.",how:"Each stroke has pathLength set to 1, so one dash offset from 1 to 0 draws any of them whatever their real length. Per-stroke custom properties set delay and duration to sequence hills, house, tree and sun. Fills fade in once the outlines finish.",use:"Hero illustrations, empty states, onboarding, feature icons, signatures.",p:"Build a self-drawing SVG illustration in one HTML file, no libraries. A simple landscape (two hill lines, a house, a tree, a sun with rays, two birds) made of stroked paths with no fill, each with pathLength=1, stroke-dasharray 1 and stroke-dashoffset 1, animated to 0 with a per-path delay --w and duration --t so they draw in order over about three seconds. After the lines, fade in a filled circle for the sun. Give the SVG role=img and a descriptive aria-label. Add a Draw again button that restarts the animations. Under prefers-reduced-motion show the finished drawing at once."},
{id:"stagger-grid",uses:["L12"],t:"Ripple stagger grid",cat:"Canvas",tone:"dark",added:"2026-11",d:"A grid of tiles where pressing any one sends a wave outward, each tile delayed by its distance from the press.",how:"Pressing a tile writes every tile's distance from it into a custom property, then restarts one keyframe animation whose delay multiplies that distance. The same number can drive scale, lift and colour, so one rule produces the whole ripple. Arrow keys move between tiles.",use:"Hero backdrops, loading screens, gallery reveals, logo walls, calendars.",p:"Build a ripple grid in one HTML file, no libraries. A 7 by 5 grid of rounded square buttons, each labelled for screen readers. On click compute for every tile d = hypot(column difference, row difference) from the pressed tile, set it as --d, then remove and re-add a class to restart a keyframe animation with animation-delay calc(var(--d) * 60ms) that scales the tile down, lifts and brightens it, and returns. Shift the grid hue a little after each wave. Arrow keys move focus across the grid. Fire one wave from the centre on load. Under prefers-reduced-motion replace the movement with a brief colour change."},
/*LAB-START*/
{"id":"arc-carousel","uses":["L08","L49"],"t":"Arc focus carousel","cat":"Carousels","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","tune":true,"d":"Cards ride an arc and the one at the top comes into focus. Drag, use the arrows or click a card; every dimension has a slider.","how":"Each card's distance from the centre becomes an angle on a circle: sine gives its x, one minus cosine gives its drop, and the same angle tilts it. The position eases toward a whole number, so a drag always settles on a card.","use":"Product pickers, team pages, feature tours, portfolios, onboarding.","p":"Build an arc carousel in one HTML file with no libraries. Nine cards (image, title, one line) are placed on a circle of radius R: for a card at distance d from the current position, angle a = d * spacing degrees, x = sin(a) * R, y = (1 - cos(a)) * R, rotation = a * tilt, scale = 1 - |d| * shrink, hidden beyond a visible count. The position is a float that eases toward an integer target every frame. Drag horizontally to move, release to snap; Left and Right arrows, Home and End work when the stage has focus; clicking a side card centres it. Show the focused card's title and line below, in a polite live region, with previous and next buttons and dots. Optional autoplay that pauses on hover and focus. Expose radius, spacing, card size, tilt, shrink, corner radius, visible count, arc direction, autoplay, accent and light or dark as settings. Respect prefers-reduced-motion by snapping without easing."},
{"id":"ring-carousel","uses":["L08"],"t":"3D image ring","cat":"Carousels","tone":"dark","added":"2026-11","tune":true,"d":"A ring of images turning in real 3D. Drag to spin it, change how many cards it holds, its radius, tilt and speed.","how":"Every card is rotated around the Y axis by its share of 360 degrees and pushed out by the radius, so together they form a cylinder. Turning the whole ring is one transform. Cards facing away are dimmed by comparing their angle with the camera.","use":"Hero showpieces, galleries, product ranges, event line-ups.","p":"Build a 3D ring carousel in one HTML file with no libraries. A scene with CSS perspective holds a ring element with transform-style: preserve-3d. N image cards are each set to rotateY(i * 360/N) translateZ(radius). The ring rotates to rotateX(tilt) rotateY(-position * 360/N), where position is a float easing toward an integer target; add a slow constant drift when autoplay is on and the pointer is away. Drag horizontally to spin, arrow keys step one card, clicking a card brings it to the front. Fade cards whose face points away from the viewer. Show the front card's title in a polite live region. Expose card count, radius, card width, perspective, tilt, drift speed, corner radius and accent as settings. With prefers-reduced-motion, no drift and no easing."},
{"id":"spotlight-carousel","uses":["L49"],"t":"Spotlight carousel","cat":"Carousels","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","tune":true,"d":"One card stands in the light with its caption; its neighbours shrink, blur and dim behind it. Tune the falloff with sliders.","how":"Cards sit in a row and overlap. A card's distance from the centre drives four things at once: how far it slides, how small it gets, how much blur it takes and how dark it goes. Only the centre card shows its text.","use":"Featured stories, testimonials with portraits, film or album pickers, case studies.","p":"Build a spotlight carousel in one HTML file with no libraries. Cards (image, title, line, small button) are absolutely placed in a row; for distance d from the current float position: x = d * (card width * overlap + gap), scale = 1 - min(|d|,2) * shrink, filter blur(min(|d|,2) * blur px) brightness(1 - min(|d|,2) * dim), z-index falls with |d|. The centre card's caption fades in; others hide theirs. Position eases to an integer target. Drag, arrow keys, click a side card to centre it, previous and next buttons and dots. Announce the centre card in a polite live region. Settings: card width, overlap, gap, shrink, blur, dim, corner radius, autoplay, accent, light or dark."},
{"id":"coverflow","uses":["L08"],"t":"Cover flow","cat":"Carousels","tone":"dark","added":"2026-11","tune":true,"d":"The classic shelf of covers: the centre one faces you, the rest turn away into depth, with an optional mirror floor.","how":"Distance from the centre is clamped to one for the turn and the push back, so every side cover shares the same angle and the row stays tidy; beyond that, covers just step sideways. A box reflection draws the floor.","use":"Album, book and film shelves, document pickers, app switchers.","p":"Build a cover flow in one HTML file with no libraries. Square covers in a perspective container; for distance d from the float position, with s = sign(d) and c = min(|d|,1): translateX = s*c*push + d*gap, rotateY = -s*c*angle, translateZ = -c*depth. The centre cover faces the viewer. Position eases to an integer; drag, arrow keys, Home and End, and clicking a cover centres it. Optional reflection with -webkit-box-reflect. Title and subtitle under the shelf in a polite live region. Settings: cover size, angle, depth, push, gap, perspective, reflection, corner radius, accent."}
/*LAB-END*/
];
